// Legal Drama: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== legal-drama.t01 | limited-series | chapters
title: The Duty Solicitor
genre: legal-drama + drama
tone: Fluorescent, humane and tired; procedure as suspense
era: - | contemporary
setting: Holloway Road Magistrates' Court, North London | fictional | court
logline: Over one bank-holiday weekend on call, a duty solicitor must decide whether to break privilege for a client she believes is confessing to protect his daughter.
premise: {farah} is the duty solicitor for a custody suite and the court above it from Friday night to Tuesday morning. She gets twenty minutes with each person the police bring in. On Friday the man in cell four, {desmond}, tells her calmly that he set the fire at his own shop. Nothing in the disclosure fits him, and everything fits {jade}, his nineteen-year-old daughter, who is in cell nine on a different charge and has asked for a different solicitor.
synopsis: Six episodes, one for each sitting and the nights between. {farah} takes instructions from {desmond}, who wants to plead guilty at the first hearing and is irritated that she keeps asking where he parked. The custody sergeant, {clive}, has known her ten years and warns her that the officer in the case, {orla}, is good and is not wrong often. {jade}, upstairs with the other duty solicitor {theo}, is charged with a public-order offence from the same night and smells of accelerant, which only {farah} noticed, in a corridor, and cannot use. The series is the rule and what it costs: she may not tell {theo} what her client said; she may not tell the court she thinks her client is lying; she may refuse to put forward a plea she knows to be false, but she does not know, she believes. Across Saturday she tests the confession the only way she is allowed to, by asking {desmond} for detail, and he fails on the shutter, which was already up. On Sunday {orla}, doing the same arithmetic from the other side, re-interviews {jade} and gets nothing, because {theo} is good too. {farah}'s own daughter, {noor}, rings from a party, wanting a lift, and {farah} hears herself give the advice she would give a client: say nothing, wait for me. On Monday, the bank holiday, the district judge {hadley} sits alone and will take a guilty plea if it is offered. {farah} tells {desmond} what she can properly tell him: that she cannot stop him pleading, that she will not mitigate on facts she believes untrue, and that a conviction for arson will not stop the police looking at who else was there. He sacks her. She is required to tell the court only that she is no longer instructed. In the finale {desmond} stands unrepresented and pleads guilty; {hadley}, who has watched {farah} leave the well of the court without a word and understood it, declines to sentence without a report and adjourns for three weeks. It is the only mercy the system has in its gift that day. {jade} is bailed. On the steps she asks {farah} what her father said, and {farah} says she can't tell her, and {jade} says, "Then he said it," and goes back inside.
question: What does a lawyer owe a client who is using her to tell a lie she cannot prove is one?
themes: privilege as a burden; a parent's right to be wrong for their child; what procedure can and cannot notice
audience: Adults who like procedure, moral pressure and no easy villains
viewpoint: With {farah} throughout; we never see the fire and never hear {jade} with her own solicitor
stakes: Six years for {desmond}, a first conviction and worse for {jade}, {farah}'s practising certificate if she steps over the line, and her standing with the one sergeant who trusts her
conflict: {farah}'s duty of confidence to a client against her belief that he is innocent, with a decent police officer closing in on the truth from the other side
ending: {desmond} pleads guilty without a lawyer; the judge adjourns rather than sentence; {jade} walks back in. Nothing is resolved in law and everything is known.
experience: The tightening of rules you come to respect, a dread of the right thing, and a last silence that says more than a verdict
look: Strip light, scuffed lino, wired glass; daylight only on the court steps; faces in interview rooms from one fixed angle each
sound: Cell doors, a tannoy calling names, a kettle in the advocates' room. No score inside the building.
m: protect | system | loyalty-vs-truth | open
rule: What a client tells his solicitor stays with her | She can stop acting; she cannot say why
rule: The duty solicitor gets twenty minutes | Every conversation ends on someone else's clock
@ farah | Farah Qureshi | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 44 | To do the job properly for whoever is in front of her | It is the one thing she has never been ashamed of | Properly, this weekend, means helping a man go to prison for nothing | Scrupulous about the rules and impatient with people who hide behind them | From trusting the rules to protect people to seeing exactly who they leave out | Quick, plain, funny when tired; never raises her voice in a cell | Suit jacket over a jumper, lanyard, biro marks on her hand
@ desmond | Desmond Achebe-Rowe | co-lead | antagonist | human | he/him | 52 | To be convicted quickly | He believes it is the last useful thing he can do for his daughter | His solicitor keeps noticing | Gentle, exact about his shop and vague about that night | From managing his lawyer to dismissing her | Courteous, unhurried, answers questions with questions | Shopkeeper's cardigan, reading glasses in his shirt pocket
@ jade | Jade Achebe-Rowe | supporting | foil | human | she/her | 19 | For it to be Tuesday | She did something on Friday she cannot say aloud | Her father has decided for her | Furious and frightened in equal parts | From silence as defiance to silence as knowledge | Clipped, sarcastic, stops mid-sentence | Hoodie, bitten nails, a hospital wristband she hasn't cut off
@ orla | Detective Sergeant Orla Brennan | supporting | rival | human | she/her | 39 | The person who actually did it | She has seen parents do this before | She has a confession on tape and a superintendent who likes it | Fair, dogged, privately on the same side as {farah} | From accepting the confession to resenting it | Level, Irish, lets silences run | Raincoat, case file held shut with an elastic band
@ clive | Sergeant Clive Marlowe | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 58 | A quiet suite and nobody hurt in it | Thirty years of weekends like this | He cannot tell her what he has seen either | By the book, with a kettle | From warning her off to holding a door a minute longer than he should | Dry, paternal, speaks in custody-record phrases | White shirt, epaulettes, a mug that says nothing on it
@ theo | Theo Mbatha | featured | foil | human | he/him | 31 | To be as good as {farah} | She trained him | He represents the other half of her problem and doesn't know it | Bright, cocky, careful where it counts | From asking her advice to pointedly not asking | Fast, charming, switches to formal when cornered | Sharp suit, trainers, two phones
@ hadley | District Judge Ruth Hadley | featured | mentor | human | she/her | 63 | A list that finishes and no injustice on her watch | She was a duty solicitor once | She can only act on what is said in front of her | Brisk on the bench, observant to a fault | From processing a list to reading a silence | Economical, courteous, lethal with a pause | Plain dark suit, no wig, a fountain pen
@ noor | Noor Qureshi | featured | none | human | she/her | 17 | A lift home and no questions | Her mother is never off duty | She is the one person {farah} could get it wrong for | Sharp, affectionate, embarrassed by her mother | Unchanged; she is what the choice would feel like | Teenage shorthand, then suddenly direct | Party clothes under a borrowed coat
~ farah - desmond | solicitor and client | he instructs her to enter a guilty plea | he dismisses her and she leaves without a word
~ farah - orla | defence and police | professional wariness | each knows what the other thinks and neither can say it
~ farah - clive | solicitor and custody sergeant | ten years of weekend shifts | he lets a consultation run over
~ farah - theo | mentor and former trainee | easy rivalry | a wall neither chose
~ desmond - jade | father and daughter | kept apart in the same building | she understands what he did from what nobody will tell her
~ farah - noor | mother and daughter | a phone call she nearly doesn't take | the advice she gives her own child
tv: 6 x 48
engine: One sitting and the night before it: a twenty-minute consultation, a rule that stops {farah} using what she has learned, one proper step she can still take, and the list moving on regardless
arc: From a confession that doesn't fit, through every lawful way of testing it, to a client who would rather have no lawyer than an honest one
ep 1 | Friday, On Call | {desmond} confesses in twenty minutes; {farah} smells accelerant on a girl in the corridor and writes nothing down
ep 2 | Saturday, First Appearance | She asks him for detail and he gets the shutter wrong; she gets the hearing put back a day on a point about disclosure
ep 3 | Saturday Night | {orla} shows her the CCTV she is entitled to see; {theo} stops asking her advice; {noor} rings for a lift
ep 4 | Sunday | {jade} is re-interviewed and says nothing; {clive} lets father and daughter pass in a corridor and regrets it
ep 5 | Bank Holiday Monday | {farah} tells {desmond} what she will and will not say for him, and he sacks her
ep 6 | Tuesday, Before The Bench | He pleads alone; {hadley} reads what {farah} did not say and adjourns; {jade} walks back in
changes: {farah} stops believing the rules are on anyone's side and keeps them anyway; {desmond} gets what he wanted and loses the one person who saw him; {jade} learns it without being told; {orla} keeps the file open; {theo} becomes the lawyer she trained
recurring: The Custody Suite; Consultation Room Two; The Advocates' Room; Court One; The Court Steps; Farah's Car
continuation: It ends here. The adjournment is the ending.
# Friday | INT | The Custody Suite | NIGHT | farah, clive | Bring her in | {clive} reads her the board: nine in, four hers. He puts cell four at the top and doesn't say why | | 3 | props=custody board+case papers
# Friday | INT | Consultation Room Two | NIGHT | farah, desmond | Take the confession | Twenty minutes. He did it, he wants to plead tomorrow, he would like to know what sentence to expect. She asks where he parked and he asks why it matters | {farah} doesn't believe her client | 7 | props=disclosure bundle+legal pad
# Friday | INT | The Custody Suite | NIGHT | farah, jade, theo, clive | Pass the other half | {jade} is walked to interview past {farah} with {theo}, who winks. A smell of petrol and smoke goes by. {farah} puts her pen down | {farah} knows something she cannot use | 3 | props=custody record
# Saturday | INT | The Advocates' Room | DAWN | farah, orla | Meet the other side | {orla} makes two teas and says the confession is very tidy. {farah} says she couldn't comment. They drink them | | 4 | props=kettle+case file
# Saturday | INT | Court One | DAY | farah, desmond, hadley, orla | Buy a day | {desmond} stands ready to plead. {farah} rises first and asks for the unused material she is entitled to; {hadley} looks at her for a moment longer than the point deserves, and puts it back to Monday | The plea is not entered | 6 | crowd=12, props=court papers
# Saturday Night | EXT | The Court Steps | DUSK | farah, noor | Give her own child the advice | On the phone to {noor}, who wants a lift from a party that has gone wrong: say nothing to anyone, wait where you are, I am coming. She hears it as she says it | | 3 | props=phone, vehicles=car
